_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Tested versions\n\nv4.4.beta1.mono.official [d33da79d3]\n\n### System information\n\nGodot v4.4.beta1.mono - Windows 10 (build 19045) - Multi-window, 3 monitors - Vulkan (Forward+) - dedicated NVIDIA GeForce GTX 1060 6GB (NVIDIA; 32.0.15.6094) - AMD Ryzen 7 1700 Eight-Core Processor (16 threads)\n\n### Issue description\n\nWhen importing a fbx file that has a material with normal map using the ufbx importer, the material's normal_scale value is set to 0.0 instead of the value that the material has when exported from the 3d modelling software. This makes the normal map invisible without manually creating a new material and overriding the imported one. If the problem is that the fbx file doesn't include a value for this, the default value should be 1.0 (which is the value that would make a baked normal map on a low poly mesh match the normals of the high poly mesh).\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\nImport an fbx file that uses a normal map, such as the one in the MRP.\n \nIn the MRP, open up test.tscn, and notice that the cube model doesn't have a visible normal map.\nSelect the Cube node (which is an editable child from the test.fbx scene).\nIn the inspector click Mesh > Surface 0 > Material > Normal Map.\nNotice that the material has a normal map set, but the normal_scale is set to 0.0, making it invisible.\n\n### Minimal reproduction project (MRP)\n\n[fbx_normal_map_issue.zip](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/18698090/fbx_normal_map_issue.zip)","closed_by":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/102502/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/102502/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null}